course: dictionary
lesson: 2474
title: Strong verbs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some verbs carry their meaning inside them. «Don't dawdle!» is what a parent or teacher says when you walk too slowly on the way to school. Dawdle already means walking or doing something too slowly and wasting time, so you do not need a helper word like "slowly".
explain: Writers often swap a weak verb plus an adverb for one strong verb. "Walked slowly" can become "dawdled", which is shorter and tells the reader more. To check a swap, put the new word into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Dawdle can change its form to show when something happens: dawdle, dawdles, dawdled, dawdling. Look at the ending to see whether it is now, in the past, or still going on.
fact: A verb is a doing word that tells you what happens.
fact: «Dawdle» means to walk or do something too slowly, wasting time.
fact: Dawdle, dawdles, dawdled and dawdling are all forms of the same verb.
fact: A strong verb can replace a weak verb plus an adverb: "walked slowly" can become "dawdled".
fact: «Don't dawdle!» is a common thing parents and teachers say on school mornings.
exercise: «Don't dawdle!» What does dawdle mean? | *To walk or do something too slowly, wasting time | To shout loudly | Run as fast as you can | To sleep in the sun
exercise: Which single verb can replace «walked slowly»? | *dawdled | jumped | shouted | sprinted
exercise: Which form of dawdle shows that it happened in the past? | dawdling | *dawdled | dawdles
test: Which word means to walk too slowly, wasting time? | race | hurry | *dawdle
test: Which form of dawdle shows that it is happening right now? | dawdled | pausing | *dawdling
exam: Replace «walked slowly» with one strong verb: which is the best choice? | sprinted | talked | *dawdled | shouted
